{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_q2ywrrvaw6jx3cwao7zpnag4rdf7auoceayrlhmjkjwjc4fcls2q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass, under every feature combination.\n\n## Result: the Rust translation needed **zero** fixes — it is byte-identical to the C\n\nNo divergence was found in any of 42 differential tests across ~5,000 randomized inputs. To make sure that wasn't a vacuous result, I mutation-tested my own harness (details below).\n\n**Phase A — surface mapped** (`SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md`)\n\nFeature combinations, derived mechanically: **exactly one (`<none>`)**. There is no `[features]` table, no optional deps, no `cfg(feature=…)` in `src/`, and no `#ifdef` in the C beyond the include guard. I additionally swept the `release` profile since `panic = \"abort\"` is a build-time config.\n\nSymbol parity: **4 C symbols, 0 missing** (`printLine`, `bad`, `good`, `driver`). One translation unit, fully translated — nothing was skipped, so no symbol needed writing. `helperBad`/`helperGood1` are `static` in C and correctly *absent* from both `.so`s.\n\n**Phases B/C/D — 42 tests, 25 `CONFIGS` rows + 12 `ERRORS` rows, all checked off**, driven only through `dlopen`/`dlsym` on both `.so`s.\n\nI verified the translation's two unusual ground-truth claims against the compiled artifact rather than trusting the comments:\n- `helperBad` returning `null` — GCC really does substitute a null pointer for the returned stack address (`mov $0x0,%eax` in the disassembly), so `bad()` prints nothing. I confirmed this holds at `-O0/-O1/-O2/-O3/-Os`, so it isn't an `-O0` artifact.\n- `printf(\"%s\\n\", …)` → `puts` — the C `.so` imports `puts`, not `printf`, even at `-O0`; both libraries write through the same libc `stdout`.\n\n## Two real bugs — both in my test harness, not the library\n\nThese are worth calling out because each would have produced a **false pass**:\n\n1. **libtest output leaked into the capture.** Capturing requires redirecting the process's fd 1, but libtest's progress printer writes there too, so `test … ok` bytes landed in the captured stream and were misreported as divergences. Fixed by flushing Rust's buffered stdout before the swap and *requiring* `RUST_TEST_THREADS=1` (the harness fails loudly rather than filtering the capture heuristically).\n\n2. **`cargo test` never rebuilds a `cdylib`.** Because integration tests don't link the library, `touch src/driver.rs && cargo test` leaves `target/*/deps/libdriver.so` untouched — the tests silently load the *previous* build. This actively masked a mutation: my first `driver`-condition mutation was reported as MISSED purely because of the stale artifact. Fixed with an mtime freshness guard that aborts the run.\n\n**Negative controls** (each applied to `src/driver.rs`, rebuilt, then restored byte-identically): `!= 0` → `== 1`; `helperBad` returning a real string; the `good` string off by one char; `printLine` skipping empty strings; `printLine` truncating to one byte; and the stale-artifact case — **all 6 detected**.\n\nI also proved the two libraries don't contaminate each other, since both export the same four names and both dispatch internal calls through the GOT/PLT: prefixing only the Rust `printLine` left the C output untouched (`C: 19 bytes` vs `Rust: 30 bytes`). That isolation is now asserted permanently by `d5_the_two_libraries_resolve_independently` — without it, every differential test could have compared one implementation against itself.\n\nRun everything with `./verify.sh` (builds C + Rust, enumerates feature combos, diffs symbols, runs all tests in dev and release). `c_src/` is unmodified — timestamps still show the original `07:32:21`; the only `Cargo.toml` change is the `libloading` dev-dependency."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":172,"cache_read_input_tokens":122610,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":172,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"972a5abf-0d1e-487d-8355-e63116185e3c","uuid":"c30fb1a2-f9ba-4523-aa06-9b215cc475f5","timestamp":"2026-08-18T07:47:45.531Z"}
